// lighttopology.granule
// part of Arcturus, a programming language and compiler for the Infocom Z-machine.
// Copyright (c) 2026, Stefan Vogt.
// https://github.com/ByteProject/Arcturus
//
// An opt-in feature: summon it with `summon.lighttopology`. Light becomes a
// LEVEL instead of a yes/no, and it travels: a lit room spills light through
// an open doorway into the room beyond, a closed door seals it off, and a
// thing can need a certain level of light to be seen at all.
//
// THE MODEL (docs/01 chapter 7). Levels are small numbers, 0 to 3 in
// practice: 0 dark, 1 dim, 2 lit (the ordinary daylight every room has
// today), 3 bright. The existing attributes keep their meaning and remain
// the switches: a room's `lit` says whether it shines by itself, a thing's
// `lit` whether it glows, and the standard library couples the glow to
// switch_on/switch_off exactly as before. `light N` sets the strength, on a
// room its own level when lit, on a thing its glow when lit; absent, both
// are light_default (2 unless the game moves it), so a game that declares
// nothing but summons the granule plays exactly as it did.
//
// Levels COMBINE BY MAXIMUM, never by sum: two candles in a crypt are
// candlelight, not daylight. The level of a room is the highest of its own
// light, the glow of anything in it (carried or lying there), and the light
// arriving from next door: each exit that leads to a room directly, or
// through a door that stands open (or is clear, a glass door), brings that
// room's own level less one. A shut opaque door brings nothing. Spill travels ONE hop: the neighbor's own
// sources count, what the neighbor itself only borrows does not, so light
// never creeps down a whole corridor of open doors.
//
// A thing with `needs_light N` is unseen in a room below level N: not
// listed, not referable, not in scope, as if hidden, until the light
// rises. What the player carries is never dimmed (hands know their
// contents, the cave rule of chapter 7), so a carried note with
// needs_light 2 stays in the inventory in the dark; examining it is still
// gated by the room being lit at all, as every examine is.
//
// HOW IT WORKS. The core asks one question, is_lit, everywhere light
// matters (scope, the room description, the light watch, the status bar,
// the darkness picture), and this granule answers it from a cached level:
// the walk over sources and exits runs once per turn and once more after
// the action, when the walk is stale (the turn-start pulse and the after
// phase mark it so) or the player has changed rooms. The core's visibility
// tests ask light_dims, the one seam this granule exists for, behind a
// compile-time fold, so a game that never summons it is byte-identical.

// The default strength: what a lit room or a glowing thing with no `light`
// of its own gives. A global, not a constant, because a game may move the
// ambient at runtime: night falls over an outdoor map (`change
// light_default to 1`), a station drops to emergency lighting, the power
// comes back (EdwardianDuck's suggestion, 2026-10-02).
global light_default = 2

// The cached level of the room the player is in, the room it was computed
// for, and the stale mark (1: recompute before the next answer).
global light_now = 0
global light_room = nothing
global light_stale = 1

// Anything may have changed light between turns (a daemon dimming a lamp,
// a timer dousing a torch), and any action may have changed it this turn
// (a switch, a door, a lamp carried in or out): mark the cache stale at
// the top of the turn and again after the action, before the light watch
// and the room description read it.
on each_turn first
    change light_stale to 1

on after other
    change light_stale to 1
    continue

// The core's one light question, answered from the level.
block is_lit()
    if light_stale is 1
        light_refresh
    else
        if light_room is not here
            light_refresh
    if light_now > 0
        return 1
    return 0

// The current level, for a game's own rules: `if light_level < 2`.
block light_level()
    if light_stale is 1
        light_refresh
    else
        if light_room is not here
            light_refresh
    return light_now

block light_refresh()
    change light_stale to 0
    change light_room to here
    change light_now to light_level_of(here, 1)

// The strength of a lit source or lit room: its `light`, or light_default.
block light_strength(obj)
    let s = obj.light
    if s is 0
        return light_default
    return s

// The level in rm: its own light, its sources, and (with spill set)
// one level less from each neighbor that an exit or an open door reaches.
// The player's carried glow counts only in the room the player stands in.
block light_level_of(rm, spill)
    let best = 0
    if rm is lit
        change best to light_strength(rm)
    if rm is here
        for each held in player
            if held is lit
                if light_strength(held) > best
                    change best to light_strength(held)
    for each near in rm
        if near is lit
            if light_strength(near) > best
                change best to light_strength(near)
    if spill is 1
        let n = exits_count
        let i = 0
        while i < n
            let d = exit_dest(rm, exit_prop(i))
            if d is not nothing
                if any_doors is 1
                    if d is door
                        // An open door passes light, and so does a shut
                        // one made of glass: `clear` means see-through
                        // everywhere in the library (a clear container
                        // shows its contents), so a clear door is a
                        // window to the light next door (EdwardianDuck's
                        // suggestion, 2026-10-02).
                        if d is open or d is clear
                            change d to door_far_side(d, rm)
                        else
                            change d to nothing
                if d is not nothing
                    let borrowed = light_level_of(d, 0) - 1
                    if borrowed > best
                        change best to borrowed
            change i to i + 1
    return best

// The visibility seam the core asks (scope, listing): is this thing too
// dim to see where the player stands? Carried things never are.
block light_dims(obj)
    let need = obj.needs_light
    if need is 0
        return 0
    if obj in player
        return 0
    if light_level < need
        return 1
    return 0
